A||||4||fiero56||Serious brake problem(long)||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-05-2000||11:44 PM||||A friend stopped by, and we decided to head to rent a video. Well, I am driving my '88 Formula down the road, and something happens(I know what, but don't want to say) that causes me to shut the engine down while still driving, I figure I can still stop the car even without power assist. I pull off the street into a parking lot, and stop the car. (I fix the problem)I start the car, and we are on our way back down the street. I notice the car is not responding to throttle inputs properly, and whenever I push in the clutch to hit another gear the RPMs drop suddenly to 400-500 and stay there until I release the clutch, and they shoot back up. I pull into the video store parking lot, push the clutch in to coast into the parking space and the engine dies. I just pull into the space, and stop without the engine on. We go in, get the movie, come back out, 5 minutes later. I start the car, it fires right up, just like normal, RPMs are good, so I pull out of the space and onto the street. I notice the car doesn't seem very powerful, so about a mile down the road I pull off, and as I push on the brake pedal(for the first time since I started it)the brakes start to grab the rotors with just the slightest pedal movement. My brakes have always had some air in them, and usually have to push the pedal down an inch before they grab. Turns out the rear passenger caliper wasn't releasing the rotor(VERY hot, and VERY smelly)so I limped it home, about 2 1/2 miles, at around 20MPH. Now it is in the driveway, and I have no clue what could be wrong. Could it be caused by my stopping the car without the power assist? Did someting in the caliper brake? Any ideas as to what happened. Both front calipers new"(rebuilt), as is the brake master cylinder. I am stumped.<P>Sorry for the length.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-06-2000||01:14 AM||||Doubt if stopping w/o power assist had anything to do with it.  How old are the flexible brake hoses?  The RR might need replacing, even if no evidence of leakage. <P>I'd raise the offending wheel, properly support the car, and go from there.  If you crack the bleeder and rotor frees up, you know it's hydraulic troubles.||||reg||1
Z||000002||capt_ron||09-06-2000||07:54 AM||||Did you use the emergency brake when you parked? Check the emergency brake cable to that wheel. If it is froze up the return spring at the caliper can't release the brake and it will drag. The lever should be forward against the stop on the caliper.<P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LAMBO||09-06-2000||09:18 AM||||My 85 se has a stuck caliper on it. This seems to be a pretty common thing with Fiero brakes. Got a replacement caliper from FieroDave to fix it. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-06-2000||12:26 PM||||Sounds like the caliper or ebrake cable just picked then to die. brake assist has nothing to do with it.<P>As for the RMP drop when you push the clutch.... that's a good one. It's posibly an electrical problem, make sure nothing is being pinched in the clutch hardware. Check all the ground wires on the block etc.<P>||||reg||1
